{
  "id": "through-the-tunnel",
  "name": "Through the Tunnel",
  "category": "security",
  "difficulty": 5,
  "xp": 400,
  "level": 10,
  "description": "SSH is blocked at the border. Get in anyway — legitimately.",
  "brief": "LAPTOP-01 is working from outside the office and needs SSH to INTERNAL-01. Firewall-01 blocks TCP/22 inbound, and that rule is there on purpose — it stays.\n\nVPN-01 sits inside the network and is listening on 1194/udp, which the firewall does allow. Traffic wrapped inside that tunnel is just UDP as far as the firewall can tell.\n\nConfigure the tunnel on LAPTOP-01: gateway 10.8.0.10, remote network 10.8.0.0/24, and give it 10.8.0.200 as its address inside the tunnel.",
  "hints": [
    "First test LAPTOP-01 → 10.8.0.20 on port 22 and see it blocked.",
    "Run 'show access-list' on Firewall-01. Which protocols survive?",
    "Open LAPTOP-01's VPN section and set it up as a client.",
    "The tunnel address must be on the remote network — try 10.8.0.200."
  ],
  "explanation": "A tunnel changes what the network in between can see. The SSH segment is still there, unchanged, but it is now the payload of an ordinary UDP datagram — and a filter matching on protocol and port has no way to look inside. The firewall was not bypassed or broken; it was shown traffic that genuinely is UDP/1194.\n\nThe tunnel address matters because the reply has to find its way home. Given an address on the internal network, the gateway answers ARP for it, so INTERNAL-01 sends replies to the gateway and they go back through the tunnel rather than leaking around it. Open the packet inspector on a tunnelled packet to see both header layers at once.",
